Better user C++ API for Dispatcher::redispatch()
Dispatcher::call() and Dispatcher::redispatch() are two API's exposed to call into the dispatcher for a particular op. Currently, Dispatcher::call() is wrapped by the C++ API, making it very simple to call. Calling redispatch() requires a little more boilerplate.
As an example, here's what's at::add() currently looks like in both its Tracing kernel (in the generated fileTraceType_2.cpp), and its Autograd kernel (in the generated file VariableType_2.cpp). Both of these kernels perform redispatches to eventually hit a backend kernel. Autograd kernels use the Dispatcher::call() API, while Tracing kernels use the Dispatcher::redispatch() API.
autograd:
Tensor add_Tensor(const Tensor & self, const Tensor & other, Scalar alpha) {
...
    return at::add(self_, other_, alpha);
...
  return result;
}

tracing:
Tensor add_Tensor(const Tensor & self, const Tensor & other, Scalar alpha) {
...
  static auto op = c10::Dispatcher::singleton()
      .findSchemaOrThrow("aten::add", "Tensor")
      .typed<Tensor (const Tensor &, const Tensor &, Scalar)>();
  auto result =c10::Dispatcher::singleton()
      .redispatch<Tensor, const Tensor &, const Tensor &, Scalar>(op, c10::DispatchKey::Tracer, self, other, alpha);
...
  return result;
}

That's a lot of boilerplate. This isn't really a major issue, since almost all usages of Dispatcher::redispatch() and its associated boilerplate are from codegen. I have a PR here though which introduces this API to a small number of non-codegen'd kernels, and it would be convenient to not have to write out as much boilerplate.
One possibility: the majority of the boilerplate comes from having to manually write out the function schema's types in .typed<...> and .redispatch<...>. One option would be a wrapper function that takes in the original function as a template parameter and deduces its return and argument types (detailed a little in this comment. Although it would have to be able to deal with function overloads correctly.
cc @ezyang @bhosmer @smessmer @ljk53 @bdhirsh @ailzhang

As a follow up to the announcement we made last year we still need Contributor License Agreements (CLAs) signed for a few collaborators. The CLA/license for PyTorch is not changing, we just have a few loose ends to tie up. If your username is not in the below list, then you don't need to do anything.
However, if your username appears on this list then we need you to sign the CLA before February 2nd:

 @alexsax
 @apaszke
