Why?
Facebook's Open Source policies require a valid CLA for an user to be a member or outside collaborator in our projects. An exemption was granted while we were adding the CLA to PyTorch. This exemption is ending on February 2nd.
What happens if I don't sign by Feb 2nd?
You will be removed from the PyTorch org and all permissions (triage, write, maintain, etc.) will be revoked. Once you sign the CLA, your permissions can be reinstated. The revocation is mostly for technical reasons, since we're managing CLA permissions with another system that makes it time-consuming to continue maintaining these exemptions.
Wait I already signed?
If you think you already signed the CLA but your name appears on this list, please reply to this issue and tag @bigfootjon to get that sorted out.

ENV

Ubuntu 16.04
MEM 16GB
LibTorch 1.7.0 with CUDA 10.2
GPU GTX 1080 Ti @ 12GB

Problem
I was building a detector demo with LibTorch, the problem encountered is that time consuming extremely high when put the tensor from CPU to GPU.
Code as follows,
STYLE 1: Tensor operations kill about 90-100 ms
// define the device
torch::DeviceType device_type;
device_type = torch::kCUDA;
torch::Device device0(device_type, 1);
// read image
cv::Mat bgr;
bgr = cv::imread("/home/ubuntu/20190701_2_00000000.jpg", cv::IMREAD_COLOR);
cv::Mat img_float_1;
bgr.convertTo(img_float_1, CV_32F, 1.0);
cv::resize(img_float_1, img_float_1, cv::Size(480, 270));
{
  torch::NoGradGuard no_grad;
  for(int idx = 0; idx < 100; ++ idx) {
    // put data into a tensor
    auto input_tensor = torch::from_blob(img_float_1.data, { 1,270,480,3 });
    input_tensor = input_tensor.permute({ 0,3,1,2 });

    // convert tensor from CPU to GPU operation kills about 90-100 ms
    input_tensor = input_tensor.to(device0);

    // forward pass kills about 2 ms
    auto outputs = module.forward({input_tensor}).toTuple();
    auto out_cls = outputs->elements()[0].toTuple();
    auto out_bbox = outputs->elements()[1].toTuple();
  }
}
STYLE 2: Tensor operations kill about 32 ms
// define the device
torch::DeviceType device_type;
device_type = torch::kCUDA;
torch::Device device0(device_type, 1);
// read image
cv::Mat bgr;
bgr = cv::imread("/home/ubuntu/20190701_2_00000000.jpg", cv::IMREAD_COLOR);
cv::Mat img_float_1;
bgr.convertTo(img_float_1, CV_32F, 1.0);
cv::resize(img_float_1, img_float_1, cv::Size(480, 270));

// define the tensor only one time
auto input_tensor = torch::from_blob(img_float_1.data, { 1,270,480,3 });
input_tensor = input_tensor.to(device0);

{
  torch::NoGradGuard no_grad;
  for(int idx = 0; idx < 100; ++ idx) {
    // tensor ops on gpu kill time about 0.024 ms
    input_tensor = input_tensor.permute({ 0,3,1,2 });

    // forward pass kills about 2 ms
    auto outputs = module.forward({input_tensor}).toTuple();
    auto out_cls = outputs->elements()[0].toTuple();
    auto out_bbox = outputs->elements()[1].toTuple();

    // feed the data into prev. tensor kills time about 32 ms
    input_tensor = torch::from_blob(img_float_1.data, { 1,270,480,3 }).to(device0);
  }
}
Here comes the question, how to reduce the convert tensor from CPU to GPU operation time cost to a reasonable value ( <= 10ms )?
Any help will be welcome, thanks a lot.
